['Here's a simplified version of how you might implement this in C. Please note that this code does not include error handling for invalid inputs (such as non-integer input) or memory allocation failures, which would require considerably more comprehensive error checking and recovery procedures. Also, it does not integrate with an external logging service due to the complexity involved. However, here's a basic implementation of reading integers from stdin into a dynamically allocated array and finding and printing the largest integer:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int* read_input(size_t *length) {
    size_t capacity = 1024;
    int *arr = malloc(capacity * sizeof(*arr));
    if (arr == NULL) exit(-1);

    *length = 0;
    while (scanf("%d", &arr[*length]) == 1) {
        (*length)++;
        if (*length >= capacity) {
            capacity *= 2;
            arr = realloc(arr, capacity * sizeof(*arr));
            if (arr == NULL) exit(-1);
        }
    }

    return arr;
}

void print_largest(int* arr, size_t length) {
    if (length == 0) return;  /* Handle edge case */

    int largest = arr[0];
    for (size_t i = 1; i < length; ++i) {
        if (arr[i] > largest) {
            largest = arr[i];
        }
    }

    printf("Largest integer is %d\n", largest);
}
```
The `read_input` function reads integers from standard input into a dynamically allocated array, doubling the capacity each time more space is needed. It updates the length as it goes along, so you can pass this to `print_largest` afterwards. The `print_largest` function then finds and prints the largest integer in the array. 

Remember that for production code, handling these edge cases would require more sophisticated error checking and recovery procedures. Also note that realloc could potentially trigger a copy of all existing data if the new size is larger than the current capacity, which may lead to performance issues as `capacity * sizeof(*arr)` can be very large. An optimized solution might involve using an explicit list structure to manage memory more carefully, or simply pre-allocating a much larger array at the beginning and only dealing with what we actually need.
']

['Here is a sample code for your request, it includes a simple logging mechanism, asynchronous flushing mechanism, and synchronization for thread safety. Note that this is just a basic example and might require adjustment based on the specifics of your use case (e.g., memory allocation failures or other system-specific details).

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <unistd.h>

#define BUFFER_SIZE 1024*1024 // adjust based on your use case
#define FLUSH_INTERVAL 5 // flush every 5 seconds

typedef struct LogEntry {
    char* message;
    size_t length;
} LogEntry;

pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
LogEntry* buffer[BUFFER_SIZE]; // circular buffer
size_t writeIndex = 0, readIndex = 0;

void asyncFlush() {
    while(1) {
        sleep(FLUSH_INTERVAL);
        pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

        // Flush the data to external service
        for (size_t i = readIndex; i != writeIndex; ++i, i %= BUFFER_SIZE) {
            LogEntry* entry = buffer[i];
            if (!entry) continue;

            printf("Sending log: %s\n", entry->message); // dummy external service call

            free(entry->message);
            free(entry);
        }

        readIndex = writeIndex = 0; // reset the buffer after flush

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
    }
}

void logMessage(const char* message, size_t length) {
    LogEntry* entry = malloc(sizeof(LogEntry)); // memory management is simplified here
    if (!entry) return;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    if ((writeIndex + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE == readIndex) {
        printf("Log buffer overflow, discarding message: %s\n", message); // buffer full, drop the message
        free(entry);
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
        return;
    }

    entry->message = malloc(length + 1);
    if (!entry->message) {
        free(entry);
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
        return;
    }

    strncpy(entry->message, message, length);
    entry->message[length] = '\0'; // null terminate the string
    entry->length = length;

    buffer[writeIndex] = entry;
    writeIndex = (writeIndex + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}

void* loggingThread(void* arg) { // dummy function for thread demonstration
    logMessage("Hello, world from thread", 23);
    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    pthread_t thread;

    if (pthread_create(&thread, NULL, loggingThread, NULL)) {
        printf("Error creating thread\n");
        return -1;
    }

    asyncFlush(); // this function will run in the main thread and flushes logs asynchronously

    pthread_join(thread, NULL);
    return 0;
}
```
This is a simple implementation for demonstration purposes. The actual implementation might require additional considerations like log rotation, error handling, better memory management strategies, etc. Please modify the code according to your needs.
']

