['Here is a basic implementation of the DocumentHandler class in C# using the .NET Framework. This code does not include unit tests, but it's a good starting point for the problem you described. Please note that this is just an example and may need to be adapted depending on your exact needs.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Xml;
using System.Threading;

public class DocumentHandler
{
    private static ReaderWriterLockSlim _envVarLock = new ReaderWriterLockSlim();

    public XmlElement FindFirstXPathElement(string xmlDocument, string xpath)
    {
        var document = new XmlDocument();
        document.LoadXml(xmlDocument);
        var nodes = document.SelectNodes(xpath);

        if (nodes == null || nodes.Count == 0)
            throw new InvalidOperationException("No elements found.");

        if (nodes.Count > 1)
            Console.WriteLine("Warning: multiple elements found. Returning the first one.");

        return (XmlElement)nodes[0];
    }

    public void CreateDirectoryWithEnvVar(string directoryName, string environmentVariableName)
    {
        _envVarLock.EnterUpgradeableReadLock();

        try
        {
            var envValue = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(environmentVariableName);

            if (envValue != null)
            {
                Console.WriteLine("Warning: the environment variable is already set.");
                return;
            }

            _envVarLock.EnterWriteLock();
            try
            {
                var directoryPath = Path.Combine(Environment.GetFolderPath(Environment.SpecialFolder.UserProfile), directoryName);

                if (Directory.Exists(directoryPath)) return;

                Directory.CreateDirectory(directoryPath);
                Environment.SetEnvironmentVariable(environmentVariableName, directoryPath);
            }
            finally
            {
                _envVarLock.ExitWriteLock();
            }
        }
        finally
        {
            _envVarLock.ExitUpgradeableReadLock();
        }
    }
}
```
This code uses the ReaderWriterLockSlim class to ensure thread-safe access to shared resources like the environment variables. The CreateDirectoryWithEnvVar method first checks if the specified environment variable is already set, and if it is, a warning is logged and the function returns early. If the environment variable is not set, it proceeds to create the directory with the given name and set an environment variable pointing to its path.

In real-world applications, you would want to handle exceptions more gracefully. For instance, the FindFirstXPathElement method should catch XmlException if there are any issues parsing the XML document, while CreateDirectoryWithEnvVar should also consider DirectoryNotFoundException and UnauthorizedAccessException. 

Unit tests for this class can be written using a testing framework like NUnit or xUnit to validate its behavior under different scenarios. This could include verifying that the FindFirstXPathElement method correctly locates elements in an XML document, that CreateDirectoryWithEnvVar correctly creates and sets up directories, and that these methods handle edge cases such as invalid XML input and race conditions during concurrent directory creation.
']

['Here is a Java code block implementing the requested features using Java's NIO package with parallel processing. However, it does not provide a complete solution due to the complexity of real world requirements such as logging framework integration or network errors handling.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.security.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.logging.*;

public class FileProcessor {
    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(FileProcessor.class.getName());
    private ExecutorService executor;

    public FileProcessor() {
        this.executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors());
    }

    public Map<String, String> processFiles(List<Path> paths) throws InterruptedException {
        ConcurrentMap<String, String> results = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
        CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(paths.size());

        for (Path path : paths) {
            executor.execute(() -> processFile(path, results, latch));
        }

        latch.await();
        executor.shutdown();
        return results;
    }

    private void processFile(Path path, Map<String, String> results, CountDownLatch latch) {
        try {
            if (Files.isRegularFile(path) && !"temp".equals(path.getFileName().toString())) {
                byte[] data = Files.readAllBytes(path);
                MessageDigest digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
                byte[] hashBytes = digest.digest(data);
                StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
                for (byte b : hashBytes) {
                    sb.append(String.format("%02x", b & 0xff));
                }
                results.put(path.toString(), sb.toString());
            }
        } catch (IOException | NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            logger.log(Level.SEVERE, "Failed to process file: " + path, e);
        } finally {
            latch.countDown();
        }
    }
}
```

This code block provides a solution with basic error handling and uses Java's NIO package for improved efficiency when reading files. It also includes logging to the console using java.util.logging, which can be integrated into any existing logging framework as needed. The thread safety of this class is guaranteed by using `ConcurrentHashMap` and `ExecutorService` from the concurrency utilities.
']

