["Here's an example solution using Node.js with the async/await pattern to make your code cleaner and handle Promise rejections gracefully. For the sake of brevity, I will only show a simplified version that reads files from a local file system and does not integrate with any external metadata service. This will require proper error handling, network resilience logic which can be added according to specific needs. 

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');
const readdirp = require('readdirp');

async function readFilesFromDir(dirPath, ext) {
    let filesData = [];
    for await (const entry of readdirp(dirPath)) {
        if (!entry.fullPath.endsWith(ext)) continue;

        try {
            const fileContent = fs.readFileSync(path.join(dirPath, entry.name), 'utf8');
            filesData.push({ path: entry.fullPath, content: fileContent });
        } catch (error) {
            console.log(`Error reading file ${entry.fullPath}. Error: ${error}`);
        }
    }
    return filesData;
}
```

However, using synchronous `fs.readFileSync()` is not recommended for large directories due to blocking of the event loop. Also, this function reads all files in memory before returning. If you are dealing with huge files or a directory structure that can have up to 10,000 files, consider reading them one by one, processing as soon as they're ready, and then freeing their memory when done.

For the metadata service part, we need integrating it into the function which is not feasible with just this code snippet without more specific requirements.

Please note that handling a large amount of files will consume a lot of memory if you try to read them all at once. A better approach would be to create an async iterator or use streams for larger amounts of data.

This solution does not handle network failures, which is something that can be added based on specific requirements and the design decisions made for such error handling. This includes retrying mechanism and timeouts etc.
