JavaScript:
```javascript
function refreshDialogFields(resourceId, url, fieldNames) {
    // Initiate a promise to handle promises sequentially instead of concurrently
    let promise = Promise.resolve();

    for (let i = 0; i < dialogFields.length; i++) {
        const fieldName = dialogFields[i].name;

        if (fieldNames.includes(fieldName)) {
            // Add a delay to simulate network latency
            promise = promise.then(() => {
                return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
                    setTimeout(() => {
                        let message = `${resourceId} ${url} ${fieldName}`;

                        parent.postMessage(message, '*');

                        // Set a timeout to handle the case where window doesn't respond within specified time period 
                        let timeout = setTimeout(() => {
                            reject(new Error('Parent window did not respond'));
                        }, 5000);

                        // Handle response from parent window
                        window.addEventListener('message', event => {
                            if (event.data === 'success') {
                                dialogFields[i].update();
                                resolve();
                            } else {
                                reject(new Error('Parent window responded with failure'));
                            }

                            clearTimeout(timeout);
                        });
                    }, 500); // Delay to simulate network latency
                })
            }).catch((error) => {
                console.log(`Error occurred: ${error}`);
                return Promise.resolve(); // Continue with the next field update
            });
        }
    }

    return promise;
}
```
C++:
```cpp
#include <forward_list>
#include <string>

void insertAfter(std::forward_list<std::string>& flist, const std::string& target, const std::string& insert) {
    auto prev = flist.before_begin();
    for (auto it = flist.begin(); it != flist.end(); ++it, ++prev) {
        if (*it == target) {
            flist.insert_after(it, insert);
            return;
        }
    }

    // If the target is not found, append to the end of list
    flist.insert_after(prev, insert);
}
```
The `refreshDialogFields` function sends a message to the parent window for each dialog field in the specified array and waits for a response before updating the next field. If the parent window doesn't respond within 5 seconds or if it responds with failure, an error is logged and the process continues with the next field update.

The `insertAfter` function searches for the first occurrence of the target string in the forward list and inserts the insertion string immediately after this occurrence. If the target string is not found, the insertion string is appended to the end of the forward list. This function operates efficiently with large lists by traversing the list only once and making a single pass through it.

To handle concurrency in C++, standard multithreading mechanisms such as locks or mutexes could be used. In JavaScript, Promises can be chained together sequentially to ensure that updates are processed one after another without overlapping. For real-time data updates from external sources, error handling and timeout should be implemented to handle potential delays or failures gracefully.
"]

