['Here is a C++ implementation that handles the aforementioned constraints. This solution uses `std::mutex` for concurrency control to ensure thread safety:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <utility>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>
#include <limits>
#include <mutex>

// Global mutex for synchronization
std::mutex mtx;

void insertOrAppendElement(std::vector<std::pair<int, std::string>>& vec, int arg1, int arg2, int arg3) 
{
    // Lock the mutex before performing any operation on vector to ensure thread safety
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

    if (vec.empty()) {
        vec.push_back({arg1, std::to_string(arg3)});
        return;
    }

    auto it = std::find_if(vec.begin(), vec.end(), [&](const auto& p) { 
        return p.first == arg1; 
    });

    if (it != vec.end()) {
        // If the pair is found, insert a new pair with the first element as second argument and second element as third argument
        vec.insert(it, {arg2, std::to_string(arg3)});
    } else {
        // If no such pair is found, append a new pair with the first element set to max of int and the second element being the third integer argument
        int max = *std::max_element(vec.begin(), vec.end());
        vec.push_back({max, std::to_string(arg3)});
    }
}
``` 
This function locks the global mutex before any operation to ensure exclusive access to the vector and protects it from concurrent modifications from different threads. It then performs a linear search for the first occurrence of the pair with the first element matching arg1 using `std::find_if`, inserting a new pair if found, otherwise appending a new pair where the first element is set as the max value in vector and second element as third argument.
']

