["Here's a possible implementation in JavaScript, using generators to manage recursion depth, avoiding excessive traversal when nodes already visited and employing a Web Worker for background computation:

```javascript
// Utility function to test if an element matches a selector
function doesMatch(element, query) {
  return element.matches(query);
}

class TraverseManager {
  constructor() {
    this.visited = new WeakSet();
    this.paused = false;
  }

  *traverse(rootElement, selector) {
    if (!this.paused && !this.visited.has(rootElement)) {
      // Mark as visited to avoid infinite recursion
      this.visited.add(rootElement);

      for (const element of rootElement.children) {
        // Yield if the current node matches the selector
        if (doesMatch(element, selector)) yield element;

        // Recursively traverse child nodes
        if (!this.paused && !this.visited.has(element)) {
          yield* this.traverse(element, selector);
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

class DOMManager {
  constructor() {
    // The Web Worker is responsible for managing the traversal
    this.worker = new Worker('web-worker.js');
    this.traverseManager = new TraverseManager();
  }

  startTraversing(rootElement, selector) {
    let traversal = this.traverseManager.traverse(rootElement, selector);

    // Send the results to the UI through a message every second
    setInterval(() => {
      const result = traversal.next();
      if (!result.done) {
        this.worker.postMessage({
          type: 'update',
          payload: Array.from(result.value), // Convert to an array for serialization
        });
      } else {
        clearInterval(this);
      }
    }, 1000);
  }
}
```

In the `web-worker.js` file, handle messages from the main thread:

```javascript
self.onmessage = (event) => {
  const message = event.data;

  if (message.type === 'update') {
    // Handle updates on the UI side
    updateUI(message.payload);
  } else if (message.type === 'pause') {
    // Pause traversal
    self.traverseManager.paused = true;
  } else if (message.type === 'resume') {
    // Resume traversal
    self.traverseManager.paused = false;
  }
};
```

This code manages a traversal using generators, which allows us to pause and resume the traversal without having to reprocess all nodes in the tree. It also uses a WeakSet for visited elements to avoid infinite loops when circular references exist in the DOM. The Web Worker is used to handle computational intensive tasks in a background thread.
"]

["To solve this problem, we can use a combination of different strategies including Module Bundling for optimization purposes, Promise.all() to ensure all modules are loaded before starting any executions, and error handling techniques for network failure scenarios. 

Please note that the actual implementation would be dependent on your existing web application framework as well as how you want to handle dynamic configuration (like environment variables or configuration files). The following is a simplified example of how such a solution might look like:

```javascript
// Array to hold our module paths
const modules = ['/path1', '/path2'];

function loadScript(src) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const script = document.createElement('script');
    script.async = true;
    script.onload = resolve;
    script.onerror = reject;
    script.src = src;
    document.head.appendChild(script);
  });
}

function loadModules() {
  Promise.all(modules.map(modulePath => loadScript(modulePath)))
    .then(() => console.log('All modules loaded successfully'))
    .catch((err) => {
      // Handle error, e.g., show a fallback UI or retry logic
      console.error('Error loading module:', err);
    });
}

loadModules();
```

In the above code, each JavaScript file in `modules` array is loaded dynamically using `loadScript()` function. If any of these scripts fail to load due to network issues, the Promise will be rejected and an error handler can then handle it appropriately (like showing a fallback UI or retrying).

