['Here's a basic implementation that satisfies most of your requirements:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.lang.*;
import org.apache.log4j.Logger;
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class PalindromeProcessor {
    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(PalindromeProcessor.class);

    private Set<String> palindromes = new ConcurrentSkipListSet<>();
    private ExecutorService executorService;

    public PalindromeProcessor() {
        this.executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors());
    }

    public void processFile(String filePath) throws IOException, ExecutionException, InterruptedException {
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

        List<Future> futures = new ArrayList<>();

        try (BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader(Paths.get(filePath), StandardCharsets.UTF_8)) {
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                Future<?> future = executorService.submit(() -> processString(line));
                futures.add(future);
            }
        }

        for (Future<?> future : futures) {
            future.get();  // Will rethrow any exceptions encountered during processing
        }

        long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        logger.info("Processing time: " + (endTime - startTime) + " ms");
    }

    private void processString(String str) {
        for (int i = 0; i < str.length(); ++i) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j <= str.length(); ++j) {
                String subStr = str.substring(i, j);

                if (isPalindrome(subStr)) {
                    palindromes.add(subStr);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    private boolean isPalindrome(String str) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = str.length() - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            if (str.charAt(left++) != str.charAt(right--)) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    public int getPalindromeCount() {
        return palindromes.size();
    }
}
```
This code reads a file line by line and processes each line in parallel using `ExecutorService`. It uses `ConcurrentSkipListSet` to keep track of unique palindromic substrings, which scales well for high throughput conditions. 

The time spent on processing is logged at the end, and logging is done outside of critical sections, so it's not impacted by synchronization overhead. It also uses `isPalindrome` method to check whether a string is palindromic or not.

Please install log4j library for this code. Also, please note that this code only process the file line by line and doesn't handle large dataset very well as it requires loading whole lines into memory which might lead to OOM errors. For handling larger datasets consider using a database or distributed computing framework such as Hadoop or Spark.
']

