    void add(const ExtendedNFAPI& item) {
        std::lock_guard<std::shared_timed_mutex> lock(mutex_);
        arr.push_back(item);
        indexMap[item.RNTI] = arr.size() - 1; // Add to index map
    }

    void update(int rnti, const ExtendedNFAPI& item) {
        std::lock_guard<std::shared_timed_mutex> lock(mutex_);
        if (indexMap.find(rnti) != indexMap.end()) {
            arr[indexMap[rnti]] = item;  // Update the element in array.
        } else {
            add(item);
        }
    }

    ExtendedNFAPI get(int rnti) const {
        std::shared_lock<std::shared_timed_mutex> lock(mutex_);  // allows concurrent reads
        if (indexMap.find(rnti) != indexMap.end()) {
            return arr[indexMap.at(rnti)];
        } else {
            throw std::out_of_range("RNTI not found in array");
        }
    }

    // Iteration over the array and operations like printing each RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG and RNTI pair.
    void iterate() const {
        std::shared_lock<std::shared_timed_mutex> lock(mutex_);  // allows concurrent reads
        for (const auto& item : arr) {
            std::cout << "RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG: " << item.RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG
                      << ", RNTI: " << item.RNTI << '\n';
        }
    }
};
```
In this code, we use a `std::shared_timed_mutex` to allow concurrent reads but exclusive write operations which helps maintain the integrity of our data under multiple threads without causing any data corruption. We also utilize a `std::map` for quick lookups and an efficient insertion/update operation.

For logging, we can add a logger object that locks its own mutex before each write operation. For handling duplicates, we could simply update the existing entry or use secondary index as described previously:

