['Here is an example implementation of a binary file loader with thread safety using C#. Due to the complexity and specific requirements, it may need further refinement based on actual usage scenario. 

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary;
using System.Threading;

public class BinaryFileLoader
{
    private readonly BinaryFormatter _formatter = new BinaryFormatter();
    private readonly ReaderWriterLockSlim _lock = new ReaderWriterLockSlim();

    public T Load<T>(string filePath) where T : class
    {
        try
        {
            _lock.EnterReadLock();

            using (FileStream fs = File.OpenRead(filePath))
            {
                return _formatter.Deserialize(fs) as T;
            }
        }
        catch (IOException ex) // Handle corrupted or incomplete files
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"IO Error: {ex.Message}");
            return null;
        }
        catch (Exception ex) 
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Unexpected error: {ex.Message}");
            throw; // Re-throw the exception after logging it.
        }
        finally
        {
            _lock.ExitReadLock();
        }
    }

    public void Save<T>(string filePath, T data) where T : class
    {
        try
        {
            _lock.EnterWriteLock();

            using (FileStream fs = File.Create(filePath))
            {
                _formatter.Serialize(fs, data);
            }
        }
        catch (IOException ex) // Handle write issues
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"IO Error: {ex.Message}");
        }
        finally
        {
            _lock.ExitWriteLock();
        }
    }
}
```
This class uses a ReaderWriterLockSlim to ensure that multiple threads can read from the file at once but only one thread can write to it at a time. This ensures thread safety during deserialization and serialization, providing efficient concurrent access to the binary file. 

For error handling, IOException is caught for corrupted or incomplete files, while other exceptions are logged and re-thrown for further processing by calling methods.

The use of ReaderWriterLockSlim allows efficient concurrent reading from the file and ensures thread safety during deserialization, improving performance. However, it may not be suitable for scenarios where write operations are frequent or there is heavy contention on the lock. It also assumes that all log data fits into memory when loading a large-scale log file which could impact the system's scalability and robustness in such situations.

Finally, this class does not integrate with an existing logging framework as it depends heavily on details of how log data is structured. Integrating it would require more information on what operations are performed on the loaded/saved log data.
']

