["Here is a simple example of how you can implement such a JavaScript function using `fetch` API along with caching mechanism to reduce redundant API calls. This code uses `localStorage` as the simplest in-memory cache solution. It also has error handling and logging, and should work seamlessly into any web application.

```javascript
let cachedUptime = null;
let lastFetchTime = 0; // Start with a time in the past to trigger an API call immediately
const API_URL = 'http://your-server.com/api/uptime';
const CACHE_DURATION = 1 * 60 * 1000; // Cache for 1 minute
const DEFAULT_UPTIME = 30 * 60; // If all else fails, return a default uptime of 30 minutes (in seconds)
let isFetching = false;

async function getUptime() {
    const now = Date.now();

    if (cachedUptime && now - lastFetchTime < CACHE_DURATION) {
        // Return cached uptime if it's fresh enough
        return cachedUptime;
    } else if (!isFetching) {
        isFetching = true;

        try {
            const response = await fetch(API_URL);

            if (response.ok) {
                const data = await response.json();
                cachedUptime = data.uptime;
                lastFetchTime = now;
                localStorage.setItem('cachedUptime', JSON.stringify({ uptime: cachedUptime, fetchTime: lastFetchTime }));
            } else {
                console.error(`Failed to fetch uptime from server. Status: ${response.status} - ${response.statusText}`);
            }
        } catch (error) {
            console.error('Error fetching uptime', error);
        } finally {
            isFetching = false;
        }
    }

    return cachedUptime || DEFAULT_UPTIME; // Use default value if there's no cached value yet
}
```

The function first checks whether the cached uptime data is fresh enough. If not, it tries to fetch the latest uptime from the server using `fetch` API. The result is stored in both cache and localStorage for future use. If fetch fails or times out, an error message will be logged and default value will be returned.

Please replace 'http://your-server.com/api/uptime' with your actual server URL. 

Also note that this is a simple example and might need modifications to suit specific requirements such as network latency handling, more complex caching strategy (e.g., LRU cache), or error handling for different types of failures. Also, it doesn't consider performance optimizations beyond the JavaScript execution environment such as server-side optimization or browser caching mechanisms like Service Workers.
"]

