['This is a complex task which requires multiple modules to address all aspects of concurrency control, data structures, logging, dependency management etc. Therefore, I'll show you an overview for some key functionalities like thread-safe list operations and basic logging system. For full implementation, we need more details and resources such as available memory size, buffer sizes, number of threads and other specific application constraints to make a decision about the actual solution.

**`PHY_RX_BUFFER_INFO_T` Structure**
```c
typedef struct {
    int RX_BUFFER_ID;
    int RX_BUFFER_SIZE;
    long long RX_BUFFER_TIMESTAMP;
} PHY_RX_BUFFER_INFO_T;
```

**Thread-safe List Operations with C11's `stdatomic.h`**
```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#define BUF_SIZE 50 //Define your buffer size as per application needs
PHY_RX_BUFFER_INFO_T _Atomic buf[BUF_SIZE];

void insert(PHY_RX_BUFFER_INFO_T info) {
    int i = 0;
    while(!atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&buf[i], NULL, &info)) 
        if (++i >= BUF_SIZE) i=0;
}

PHY_RX_BUFFER_INFO_T delete(int id) {
    int i = 0;
    PHY_RX_BUFFER_INFO_T old = {};
    for (; i < BUF_SIZE; ++i){
        if(buf[i].RX_BUFFER_ID == id && atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&buf[i], &old, NULL)) 
            return old;
    }
}
```
These two operations are thread-safe because `atomic_compare_exchange_strong` will only perform the swap if another thread hasn't already modified the memory location between its check and execution. This provides a form of mutual exclusion where no two threads can operate on the same buffer slot at the same time.

**Logging with Basic C11 `<stdio.h>`**
```c
void log_operation(char *op, PHY_RX_BUFFER_INFO_T info){
    FILE *logfile = fopen("module.log", "a");
    if (logfile == NULL) {
