['Here is an example implementation for this task. This code uses a linked list structure to store the `NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T` structs, which are dynamically allocated as needed. The allocation function checks if the system's available memory can accommodate a new structure before allocating more. To adjust the maximum number of structures based on resource availability and usage patterns, it implements an adjustable threshold mechanism to determine when to stop adding new nodes to the list:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <sys/resource.h>
#include <errno.h>

typedef struct RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG {
    // add your fields here
} RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG;

typedef struct CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG {
    int CRCFLAG; 
    // add your fields here
} CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG;

typedef struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG rxInfoTag;
    int RNTI;
    CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG crcIndicationRel8Tag;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

typedef struct Node {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T data;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL; // start of the list
int currentStructures = 0;  // counter for number of structures in the list
int maxStructuresThreshold; // adjustable threshold to adjust based on resource availability and usage patterns

void populateList() {
    srand(time(NULL)); // seed the random generator

    struct rlimit lim = { .rlim_cur = 1024 * 1024, .rlim_max = 1024 * 1024 }; // set memory limit to 1MB for example
    if (setrlimit(RLIMIT_AS, &lim) == -1){
        perror("Error adjusting memory limit");
        return;
    }

    while(1) {
        struct rlimit lim;
        getrlimit(RLIMIT_AS, &lim); // get current memory usage

        if (lim.rlim_cur - 1024 < sizeof(Node)) { 
            // if available memory is less than one node, we should stop adding nodes to the list
            break;
        } else if (currentStructures >= maxStructuresThreshold) {  
            // if structures count reaches threshold, we should stop adding nodes to the list
            break;
        }

        Node* newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node)); 
        if (newNode == NULL) { 
            perror("Memory allocation failed");
            break;
        }

        // Populate NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T structure with random values
        newNode->data.RNTI = rand();
        newNode->data.crcIndicationRel8Tag.CRCFLAG = rand() % 2;

        newNode->next = head;
        head = newNode;
        currentStructures++;
    }
}
```
In this code, `maxStructuresThreshold` should be adjusted based on the specific needs of your application and how much memory you are willing to allocate. The `populateList` function will continue to add nodes until it reaches the maximum limit for structures or there is not enough memory available. Please note that in a real-world scenario, you'd likely need to handle more complex scenarios such as checking available disk space and handling other resource constraints.
