["Here is a simple implementation of `allocateAndPopulateBuffers` in C using pthreads for synchronization and stdlib for random allocation sizes. This code does not include error handling, logging, integration with external services, profiling or optimization beyond basic memory management and thread safety. 

This would be part of a larger system so it's just the function itself:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct {
    int buffer_id;
    size_t buffer_size;
    void *data_pointer;
} BUFFER_POOL;

static pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

BUFFER_POOL* allocateAndPopulateBuffers(int num, int minSize, int maxSize) {
    if (num <= 0 || minSize < 1 || maxSize < minSize) return NULL; // error handling

    srand(time(NULL)); 
    BUFFER_POOL *pool = malloc(num * sizeof(BUFFER_POOL));
    if (!pool) return NULL; // error handling

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    for (int i = 0; i < num; i++) {
        pool[i].buffer_id = i; 
        pool[i].buffer_size = rand() % (maxSize - minSize + 1) + minSize; // random size within range

        void *data_pointer = malloc(pool[i].buffer_size);
        if (!data_pointer) { // error handling
            free(pool);
            return NULL;
        } 

        pool[i].data_pointer = data_pointer;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return pool;
}
```
This code provides basic error checking for invalid parameters, and ensures thread safety via a global mutex. It allocates an array of buffers and each buffer's `data_pointer` is allocated with memory equal to its `buffer_size`. If any allocation fails, the function frees all previously allocated memory before returning NULL.

Remember to free all allocated memory when you are done using it:

```c
void cleanUp(BUFFER_POOL* pool, int num) {
    if (pool == NULL) return;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    for (int i = 0; i < num; i++) free(pool[i].data_pointer); 
    free(pool);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
The above code assumes that you would call `cleanUp` at the end of your program to free all allocated memory. This is a good practice to prevent memory leaks. Also, it uses `pthread_mutex_lock/unlock` for thread safety, as we are modifying shared state (the `pool` array) and need to ensure that two threads do not try to access or modify the same buffer simultaneously. 

Please note that this is a very basic implementation, in a real-world situation, much more would be needed to handle all aspects of the problem including error handling, logging, integration with external services, profiling and optimization, etc.
